#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111783} :activity/id #uuid "0008051f-7603-507f-b160-f673aeb16997" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041 :topic/id #uuid "7cb38e4f-6e1e-50f0-9388-bd5b8a4168e2" :topic/math-academy-id 1515} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111851 :tutorial/content "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$. We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB^(―) & ≅XY^(―), & AC^(―) & ≅XZ^(―), & BC^(―) & ≅YZ^(―), \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A, & ∠Y & ≅∠B, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write\n$ABC \\cong XYZ$.\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6be6893-a7ca-578a-9510-8a8c2166e89b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2293 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a10c82d4-3ea7-5197-929f-a0a7a5910276" :step/math-academy-id 9553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110419 :question/id #uuid "aedb6e30-d1e6-5d89-aa83-56c1d6946b50" :question/math-academy-id "e-2904"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111064} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759